speechtext:
But the Opposition is correct in one respect. We could have structured this transaction to get a higher sale price, but only if we had been prepared to sacrifice the interests of Canadians by allowing higher overseas telephone rates, or allowing the new buyer to charge monopoly rates, or if we had been prepared to allow foreign bidders who might have bid higher. The Opposition may want Canadian consumers to pay higher phone rates to support monopoly profits, they may want foreign ownership, but this Government does not, Mr. Speaker.
The final substantive part of this Bill is Part VII, Sections 24 through 33. I have already described the interim regulatory regime. Much of the rest of this part is concerned with the provisions we are making for employees.

In legal terms, these clauses provide that all collective agreements will remain in force and they also provide for the transfer of pension plans.
A number of benefits and numerous guarantees are included in the sale of Teleglobe to Memotec, more specifically:
continuity of bargaining agreements;
creation of a new pension plan for present Teleglobe employees that is comparable in every detail to the plan now in force in the Federal Public Service;
Teleglobe Canada
within six months, the opportunity for Teleglobe employees to purchase five per cent of Teleglobe's shares at 90 per cent of the price paid by Memotec, through interest-free loans repayable over five years;
a long-term share purchase plan at a later date;
eligibility of the employee share ownership program for the purposes of the Quebec Stock Savings Plan, if possible;
and maintaining the corporation's headquarters in Montreal, as I mentioned earlier.

This is yet another item to add to the mounting pile of evidence that all points to the fact that privatization is a good deal for employees as well as for everybody else. Take, for example, the sale of de Havilland to Boeing. We all remember the cries of anguish from the Opposition benches during the sale of de Havilland to Boeing and the Opposition's predictions of doom and gloom and the sky falling in. But we hear nothing now from these particular "Chicken Littles" because the sale has been good for de Havilland. It has been good for the Canadian aircraft industry. It has been good for Canadian workers. The sale has been held as a natural partnership by industry experts around the world.
